WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.459
Overdoses, suicidal

00:00:01.459 --> 00:00:03.712
people, domestic violence.

00:00:03.712 --> 00:00:04.421
What does the public

00:00:04.421 --> 00:00:06.256
have a right to hear?

00:00:06.256 --> 00:00:06.923
Where is the line

00:00:06.923 --> 00:00:08.174
between public information

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:09.676
and an invasion of privacy

00:00:09.676 --> 00:00:11.886
when it comes to police scanners?

00:00:11.886 --> 00:00:13.471
For decades, police departments

00:00:13.471 --> 00:00:15.432
have communicated through public radio

00:00:15.432 --> 00:00:17.350
to dispatch their officers,

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:19.185
attracting a relatively small number

00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:20.353
of listeners.

00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:22.397
Technology has made it much easier.

00:00:22.397 --> 00:00:25.400
In 2012, with the launch of Broadcast Ify

00:00:25.567 --> 00:00:26.651
the largest platform

00:00:26.651 --> 00:00:28.653
for streaming live audio,

00:00:28.653 --> 00:00:29.821
the company currently offers

00:00:29.821 --> 00:00:31.448
more than 7600

00:00:31.448 --> 00:00:33.700
live audio feeds in different cities

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:34.868
across the country

00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:36.286
for police, for fire,

00:00:36.286 --> 00:00:37.454
EMS aircraft,

00:00:37.454 --> 00:00:40.123
marine and amateur radio communications.

00:00:40.123 --> 00:00:40.749
Broadcast

00:00:40.749 --> 00:00:42.792
Ify makes their feeds easy

00:00:42.792 --> 00:00:44.377
to access through its website

00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:45.587
and smartphone apps,

00:00:45.587 --> 00:00:46.171
reaching

00:00:46.171 --> 00:00:48.131
50 to 60000 listeners

00:00:48.131 --> 00:00:50.175
on a typical weekday evening.

00:00:50.175 --> 00:00:51.801
Ultimately, the platform offers

00:00:51.801 --> 00:00:53.053
the public transparency

00:00:53.053 --> 00:00:53.803
to what's going on

00:00:53.803 --> 00:00:54.804
in their own backyards.

00:00:54.804 --> 00:00:57.057
But is there a cost?

00:00:57.057 --> 00:00:57.724
Major cities,

00:00:57.724 --> 00:00:59.267
including Denver, San Francisco,

00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:01.853
San Diego, Baltimore, Chicago, New York

00:01:01.853 --> 00:01:02.937
and Sioux Falls

00:01:02.937 --> 00:01:04.022
are now encrypting

00:01:04.022 --> 00:01:06.274
their emergency public radio channels

00:01:06.274 --> 00:01:08.318
due to concerns of oversharing details

00:01:08.318 --> 00:01:09.611
about private civilians

00:01:09.611 --> 00:01:10.445
with a much wider

00:01:10.445 --> 00:01:11.946
audience publicizing

00:01:11.946 --> 00:01:13.740
names and addresses and phone numbers.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:16.743
It worries some in public safety.

00:01:16.743 --> 00:01:18.244
Encrypting radio channels

00:01:18.244 --> 00:01:19.120
from the public

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:20.663
would mean that only authorized

00:01:20.663 --> 00:01:21.998
emergency professionals

00:01:21.998 --> 00:01:23.416
could access this information

00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:26.086
about an urgent matter in real time.

00:01:26.086 --> 00:01:27.378
We found several examples.

00:01:27.378 --> 00:01:28.171
For example,

00:01:28.171 --> 00:01:30.340
this an elderly man in Wisconsin

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:34.010
who was in distress.

00:01:34.094 --> 00:01:35.345
For a 70 year old male that

00:01:35.345 --> 00:01:39.265
fell in the kitchen, unable to get up.

00:01:39.307 --> 00:01:40.475
Of course, one could argue

00:01:40.475 --> 00:01:41.351
that police scanners help

00:01:41.351 --> 00:01:42.936
the public identify individuals

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:45.021
that might be a danger to themselves

00:01:45.021 --> 00:01:47.440
or to others.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.150
Even violent, possibly mental

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:52.153
elderly black male subjects.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:56.032
I think he's walking with a stick or cane

00:01:56.116 --> 00:01:57.408
is going to be in front of the store

00:01:57.408 --> 00:01:59.953
for an all black clothing.

00:01:59.953 --> 00:02:01.579
But at what point does a radio channel

00:02:01.579 --> 00:02:03.331
that's meant to uphold public safety

00:02:03.331 --> 00:02:04.791
infringe on the private details

00:02:04.791 --> 00:02:10.171
of someone's, say, mental health?

00:02:10.255 --> 00:02:11.172
If he says

00:02:11.172 --> 00:02:13.383
he feels like committing suicide,

00:02:13.383 --> 00:02:14.300
the final thoughts

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.053
and feelings fight with

00:02:17.053 --> 00:02:20.056
her husband cheating on her.

00:02:20.056 --> 00:02:21.099
Apart from the privacy

00:02:21.099 --> 00:02:22.725
considerations, might the widespread

00:02:22.725 --> 00:02:24.352
broadcasting of personal details

00:02:24.352 --> 00:02:25.812
cause hesitance

00:02:25.812 --> 00:02:27.438
in someone who needs help

00:02:27.438 --> 00:02:29.399
before dialing 911?

00:02:29.399 --> 00:02:29.774
Joining me

00:02:29.774 --> 00:02:31.234
now is the founder of broadcast

00:02:31.234 --> 00:02:32.569
to Fi Blanton.

00:02:32.569 --> 00:02:33.444
He's also the founder

00:02:33.444 --> 00:02:35.697
and CEO of radio reference.

00:02:35.697 --> 00:02:36.948
Lindsay, thank you for being here.

00:02:36.948 --> 00:02:38.950
This used to be a niche hobby

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:40.660
on my street lived.

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:42.120
The fire chief

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:43.121
Chief Hall Chief

00:02:43.121 --> 00:02:44.914
Karl Hall, wonderful guy.

00:02:44.914 --> 00:02:46.040
Like they were the only ones

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:47.750
that I remember as having a scanner.

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:49.085
But now

00:02:49.085 --> 00:02:50.503
lots of folks have access,

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:52.005
including through your website.

00:02:52.005 --> 00:02:54.716
What changed?

00:02:54.757 --> 00:02:56.092
Well, obviously,

00:02:56.092 --> 00:02:57.844
the Internet and streaming

00:02:57.844 --> 00:03:00.471
changed the hobby that, you know,

00:03:00.471 --> 00:03:04.434
we grew up with almost 50 years ago,

00:03:04.475 --> 00:03:05.977
listening in to our local public

00:03:05.977 --> 00:03:07.270
safety agencies

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:08.354
to bring it online,

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:10.815
just like all streaming services.

00:03:10.815 --> 00:03:11.441
So that's really

00:03:11.441 --> 00:03:12.525
what's changed

00:03:12.525 --> 00:03:13.318
in the hobby

00:03:13.318 --> 00:03:14.485
for those that listen

00:03:14.485 --> 00:03:16.696
to police scanners today.

00:03:16.696 --> 00:03:18.114
Can we make it clear that

00:03:18.114 --> 00:03:20.241
there's already some level of information

00:03:20.241 --> 00:03:22.660
that is not accessible, not shared,

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:23.828
a SWAT team.

00:03:23.828 --> 00:03:25.747
And by the way, Ernest London,

00:03:25.747 --> 00:03:26.998
you wrote a great piece

00:03:26.998 --> 00:03:28.666
in The New York Times about this.

00:03:28.666 --> 00:03:29.584
But explain

00:03:29.584 --> 00:03:33.963
what's off limits as things stand now.

00:03:34.047 --> 00:03:36.507
Yes, most agencies encrypt highly

00:03:36.507 --> 00:03:37.800
sensitive information,

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:39.510
such as SWAT team operations

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:41.304
and surveillance operations,

00:03:41.304 --> 00:03:42.388
or they'll use alternate

00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:43.514
means of communication

00:03:43.514 --> 00:03:46.476
like cell phones or other other

00:03:46.643 --> 00:03:47.977
other methods to communicate

00:03:47.977 --> 00:03:49.562
these highly sensitive operations.

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:51.522
But for the general routine

00:03:51.522 --> 00:03:52.899
dispatch operations,

00:03:52.899 --> 00:03:54.400
most public safety agencies

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:55.985
leave that in the clear

00:03:55.985 --> 00:03:57.070
for the public to monitor

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:58.863
because there is a benefit to that, to

00:03:58.863 --> 00:04:00.365
the overall general public

00:04:00.365 --> 00:04:01.574
understanding the day to day

00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:04.285
operations of their public safety.

00:04:04.327 --> 00:04:05.370
And I'm one who

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:06.496
believes that, you know, as

00:04:06.496 --> 00:04:07.830
they say, sunshine is a

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:09.207
is a great disinfectant.

00:04:09.207 --> 00:04:10.959
I like the public being able

00:04:10.959 --> 00:04:11.459
to provide

00:04:11.459 --> 00:04:12.794
some oversight role

00:04:12.794 --> 00:04:14.754
relative to public servants.

00:04:14.754 --> 00:04:16.923
But will you speak to some of the privacy

00:04:16.923 --> 00:04:18.424
concerns and considerations

00:04:18.424 --> 00:04:19.425
that I laid out

00:04:19.425 --> 00:04:22.011
in introducing this segment?

00:04:22.011 --> 00:04:23.054
Yeah, yeah, sure.

00:04:23.054 --> 00:04:25.431
It's definitely a concern.

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:28.935
Most agencies, though, do not

00:04:29.018 --> 00:04:31.604
broadcast names and addresses

00:04:31.604 --> 00:04:34.440
and specific personal information over

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:35.650
unencrypted airwaves.

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:38.027
They'll typically take that to channels

00:04:38.027 --> 00:04:40.780
that broadcast FDA does not allow.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:42.365
So it'd be a lot more different,

00:04:42.365 --> 00:04:43.992
difficult for the general public

00:04:43.992 --> 00:04:46.035
to be able to hear that information.

00:04:46.035 --> 00:04:47.578
So the privacy concerns are there?

00:04:47.578 --> 00:04:50.164
Certainly. Yeah.

00:04:50.164 --> 00:04:51.374
What level of concern

00:04:51.374 --> 00:04:52.583
do you have about this

00:04:52.583 --> 00:04:54.377
trend toward encryption?

00:04:54.377 --> 00:04:55.962
Do you think that it's gone too far?

00:04:55.962 --> 00:04:58.131
I rattled off some of the larger areas

00:04:58.131 --> 00:04:58.965
that are already headed

00:04:58.965 --> 00:05:01.509
in this direction.

00:05:01.592 --> 00:05:02.677
You know,

00:05:02.677 --> 00:05:03.845
public safety agencies

00:05:03.845 --> 00:05:05.263
have had encryption technology

00:05:05.263 --> 00:05:08.266
available to them for well over 40 years.

00:05:08.266 --> 00:05:09.559
There's been a trend to do that.

00:05:09.559 --> 00:05:10.393
But broadcast

00:05:10.393 --> 00:05:12.270
it was also seen a trend

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:13.563
of some of the largest cities

00:05:13.563 --> 00:05:14.230
in the countries

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:15.940
who have gone to encryption

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:18.067
actually make their live audio

00:05:18.067 --> 00:05:19.485
available to the general public

00:05:19.485 --> 00:05:20.194
to broadcast

00:05:20.194 --> 00:05:23.448
to five cities like Chicago and Baltimore

00:05:23.531 --> 00:05:25.283
who just recently encrypted their day

00:05:25.283 --> 00:05:26.743
to day communications.

00:05:26.743 --> 00:05:27.910
They provided delayed

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:29.746
like audio feed to broadcast

00:05:29.746 --> 00:05:31.622
because they see the benefit

00:05:31.622 --> 00:05:32.457
of the community

00:05:32.457 --> 00:05:33.416
hearing the day to day

00:05:33.416 --> 00:05:37.462
operations of their agencies.

00:05:37.545 --> 00:05:39.881
When I read the story about this subject

00:05:39.881 --> 00:05:41.132
and started to think about it,

00:05:41.132 --> 00:05:42.967
I guess the greatest concern that I had

00:05:42.967 --> 00:05:45.970
is that it might cause hesitancy

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:48.014
on the part of somebody who needs help

00:05:48.014 --> 00:05:48.681
because maybe it's

00:05:48.681 --> 00:05:49.849
an embarrassing predicament

00:05:49.849 --> 00:05:50.850
or maybe they worry

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:51.726
that it will be perceived

00:05:51.726 --> 00:05:52.518
as embarrassing.

00:05:52.518 --> 00:05:53.561
Maybe it's mental health,

00:05:53.561 --> 00:05:54.562
maybe it's domestic.

00:05:54.562 --> 00:05:55.813
I don't know what it is,

00:05:55.813 --> 00:05:56.981
but you don't want somebody

00:05:56.981 --> 00:06:00.360
not to dial 911 who's in distress.

00:06:00.401 --> 00:06:02.653
Your thoughts?

00:06:02.737 --> 00:06:03.321
You know,

00:06:03.321 --> 00:06:04.155
it's no different

00:06:04.155 --> 00:06:07.158
than the media publicizing an event or,

00:06:07.241 --> 00:06:08.409
you know,

00:06:08.409 --> 00:06:10.870
covering a story about somebody.

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:12.246
We're providing a service

00:06:12.246 --> 00:06:14.624
to the general public to keep them aware,

00:06:14.624 --> 00:06:16.417
keep them up to date on

00:06:16.417 --> 00:06:17.085
what's going on

00:06:17.085 --> 00:06:18.711
in their local communities.

00:06:18.711 --> 00:06:21.339
You know, at times during major events,

00:06:21.339 --> 00:06:22.590
hundreds of thousands of people

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:23.591
will tune in to want to know

00:06:23.591 --> 00:06:24.926
what's going on.

00:06:24.926 --> 00:06:28.221
So there's a public good to

00:06:28.304 --> 00:06:29.555
the general public

00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:30.098
knowing what

00:06:30.098 --> 00:06:31.057
their local public

00:06:31.057 --> 00:06:32.266
public safety agencies

00:06:32.266 --> 00:06:34.143
are doing on a day to day basis.

00:06:34.143 --> 00:06:37.230
And those agencies, they they they know

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:39.065
that general public's listening

00:06:39.065 --> 00:06:40.400
and they're cognizant of that.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:42.485
So.

00:06:42.485 --> 00:06:43.194
Lindsay Blanton,

00:06:43.194 --> 00:06:44.445
thank you so much for being here.

00:06:44.445 --> 00:06:47.156
Appreciate it. Thank you, Michael.

